  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia has supported Indonesia&#x2019;s health system through enhanced information systems since 2021. By 2023, in collaboration with Indonesia&#x2019;s Ministry of Health, Australia has furthered the development and implementation of the Secure and Interoperable Surveillance and Health Information System (SISHIS) in Badung and Buleleng district of Bali. A notable achievement in mid-2024 was that both districts officially committed to adopting SISHIS with their own resources. This commitment aligns with Indonesia&#x2019;s health transformation agenda, empowering local health managers to consolidate and analyse data from multiple systems for informed decision-making.
&#x200d;
Australia also strengthened Indonesia&#x2019;s national animal health information system (iSIKHNAS). Throughout 2023 and 2024, iSIKHNAS has been crucial for animal health authorities in identifying threats, setting priorities, and responding to outbreaks like Foot-and-Mouth Disease (FMD) and rabies. These efforts highlight Australia&#x2019;s vital role in improving Indonesia&#x2019;s health security infrastructure.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported Indonesia in preparing for the International Health Regulations Joint External Evaluation (IHR JEE) in 2023, bringing together multisectoral stakeholders to identify gaps in coordination and policy frameworks. Regional collaboration was enhanced through the development of ASEAN and Indonesian national One Health Joint Plans of Action. By integrating disaggregated data on sex and disabilities in Public Health Emergency Operations Centre (PHEOC) reports, Australia ensured visibility of women and vulnerable populations, enabling targeted community engagement and outbreak response interventions, promoting health equity.
&#x200d;
Australia also developed guidelines and a training curriculum for community-based surveillance, aligning with international standards to improve evidence-based policymaking. An evaluation of Indonesia&#x2019;s surveillance capacities highlighted the need for expanded early warning and event-based surveillance in priority provinces. This effort bolstered detection and reporting at the community level and strengthened coordination with development partners.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported UNICEF to collaborate with the Ministry of Home Affairs in West Nusa Tenggara province, on a primary health care (Puskesmas) strengthening pilot, focused on strengthening governance, improving planning and budgeting, utilizing data for evidence-based decision-making, strengthening supportive supervision systems, fostering continuous quality improvement, and introducing entrepreneurship skills to attain a non-profit local public service (BLUD) status. 15 new local regulations were issued. Over 1300 (623 males 667 females) staff were trained. Having initially involved 55 Puskesmas, the initiative has now been expanded to an additional 31 Puskesmas in 2024. 52 out of 55 Puskesmas achieved the highest level of accreditation. Puskesmas Gerung earned the prestigious 2023 Public Service Delivery Compliance Assessment award from the Indonesian Ombudsman, highlighting the local government&#x2019;s commitment to exceptional service standards. This recognition reflects the strategic use of the BLUD budget, ensuring top-tier care and positive patient experiences, through meticulous local specific fund allocation.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia through UNICEF assisted the Ministry of Health to develop a mobile application and chatbot called ASIK, to record and store individual case records in a central database and analyse data via a web-based dashboard. ASIK enabled local health authorities to monitor vaccination coverage rates, identify any gaps in immunization efforts and take corrective action to ensure that all children are vaccinated. Since its launch, the ministry has moved from paper-based to electronic immunisation records and mandated the use of ASIK by all district health facilities. ASIK has collected the individual immunisation records of 21,895,882 children by January 2023</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia with South Sulawesi provincial government supported equitable and inclusive vaccine distribution. The aim was to trial inclusive vaccine coverage through advocacy, multi-stakeholder coordination (government, disabled people organisations, communities, media, private sector), and inclusive mass vaccination events, including the establishment of vaccination centers that provided access to people with disability. Guidelines for health staff on how to interact with people with disability promoting lasting outcomes were endorsed. The support was initially implemented in five districts as a pilot in July 2022 and the government agreed to scale the approach to all 24 districts in the province.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through WHO, enhanced Indonesia&apos;s preparedness and response to infectious diseases. The trial of a risk mapping tool for Hantavirus, Nipah virus, and COVID-19 led to its widespread adoption by provincial health authorities. Australia also supported the procurement of 3,080 rabies serums and 8,850 rabies vaccines for high-risk provinces. Additionally, Australia funded a Continuous Quality Improvement assessment of Five Epidemiology Training Program (FETP) universities, informing a national curriculum improvement strategy.
&#x200d;
Australia&apos;s support for Indonesia&#x2019;s Public Health Emergency Operations Centre (PHEOC) and Early Warning Alert and Response System (EWARS), including Zoonoses Surveillance Information System (SIZE) and WHO&#x2019;s Epidemic Intelligence from Open Sources (EIOS), resulted in a tenfold increase in outbreak detection, with 33 of 38 provinces achieving &#x2265; 80% alert response. EWARS implementation has expanded to primary healthcare centres, hospitals, laboratories, and Points of Entry (PoEs). Australia also improved PoE cross-border coordination, enhancing information-sharing networks between Indonesia and Timor-Leste.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Australia Indonesia Health Security Partnership (AIHSP) worked in partnership with the Bali Provincial Government to improve rabies disease control, providing strategic technical and operational advice.
&#x200d;
AIHSP supported Bali to expand Rabies Alert village Teams (TISIRA) to the remaining six Districts (after a pilot phase covering three). TISIRA builds on the Ministry of Agriculture&#x2019;s &#x2018;Cadre model&#x2019; to connect village and religious leaders, community members and local services to work closely with animal and human health services. It educates communities about rabies, improves dog bite case management, and supports rabies vaccinations. Two hundred thousand dog vaccines were also handed over to the Bali Government in August 2023.
&#x200d;
These efforts supported the leadership and action of the Balinese Government, contributing to a reduction in the number of rabies deaths in Bali from 22 to four (2022-23 to 2023-24).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Between July and October 2024, the Australia Indonesia Health Security Partnership (AIHSP) consolidated and institutionalised its health service capacity-building efforts to strengthen Indonesia&#x2019;s ability to deliver quality health services. As part of this, AIHSP supported the rollout of Training of Trainers (ToT) modules on emerging infectious disease (EID) preparedness, risk communication, and the One Health approach.
&#x200d;
These modules have now been formally adopted by the Ministry of Health as part of its national training system and have also been integrated into the national health curriculum. The training is mandatory for all primary healthcare personnel, helping to standardise competencies across the health workforce and improve frontline service delivery, particularly at the Puskesmas (community health centre) level.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
